  "problem": "Environmental scientists are studying the population of fish in a lake.\nThey observe that the fish population increases due to reproduction at a rate $r(t)$ given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=40{t}^{2}+6t,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in fish per month, and $t>0$ is the time in months since the study began.\nAt the same time, $10$ fish are lost each month due to predation.\n\nIf there were initially $500$ fish in the lake, find the expression that gives $P(t),$ the number of fish in the lake $t$ months after the beginning of the study.\n\n$P(t)=\\frac{40}{3}{t}^{3}\\,+$\n\n{{field-1}}",
